1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:03,049
It's two a.m. You're almost asleep.

2
00:00:03,089 --> 00:00:06,258
And then, out of nowhere, your brain hits

3
00:00:06,298 --> 00:00:09,076
play. The thing you said at a party...

4
00:00:09,116 --> 00:00:11,346
six years ago. Word for word.

5
00:00:11,386 --> 00:00:15,101
And you actually wince, alone, in the dark.

6
00:00:16,151 --> 00:00:18,916
Your brain can't remember where you put your

7
00:00:18,956 --> 00:00:21,785
keys this morning. But it kept a perfect copy

8
00:00:21,825 --> 00:00:24,398
of that. And it's ready to play it again...

9
00:00:24,438 --> 00:00:25,426
and again.

10
00:00:26,476 --> 00:00:28,084
Here's the strange part.

11
00:00:28,124 --> 00:00:30,694
Replaying it feels like you're working

12
00:00:30,734 --> 00:00:33,784
something out. But in experiments, people who

13
00:00:33,824 --> 00:00:36,943
kept turning a bad feeling over often ended up

14
00:00:36,983 --> 00:00:40,492
feeling worse… and solving problems worse too.

15
00:00:41,542 --> 00:00:43,888
And yet, one team of researchers found a way

16
00:00:43,928 --> 00:00:46,219
to think about the very same kind of memory

17
00:00:46,259 --> 00:00:48,279
that took a lot of the heat out of it.

18
00:00:48,319 --> 00:00:49,852
It isn't about thinking less.

19
00:00:49,892 --> 00:00:52,519
It's about where you stand when you think.

20
00:00:58,369 --> 00:00:59,868
First, the replay itself.

21
00:00:59,908 --> 00:01:02,701
Psychologists call these involuntary memories

22
00:01:02,741 --> 00:01:05,534
memories that pop into your head on their own

23
00:01:05,574 --> 00:01:08,079
without you going looking for them.

24
00:01:09,129 --> 00:01:11,184
In the nineteen nineties, the Danish

25
00:01:11,224 --> 00:01:13,804
psychologist Dorthe Berntsen started studying

26
00:01:13,844 --> 00:01:16,423
them, partly by having people keep diaries of

27
00:01:16,463 --> 00:01:18,984
them. Most of the students she surveyed said

28
00:01:19,024 --> 00:01:21,819
they had them at least a few times a week.

29
00:01:22,869 --> 00:01:24,874
And most of them weren't random.

30
00:01:24,914 --> 00:01:26,600
They were set off by a cue

31
00:01:26,640 --> 00:01:29,539
a place, an object, a phrase, a feeling in the

32
00:01:29,579 --> 00:01:30,952
body… or a mood.

33
00:01:32,002 --> 00:01:34,412
So at two a.m., the trigger can be tiny.

34
00:01:34,452 --> 00:01:36,004
A song stuck in your head.

35
00:01:36,044 --> 00:01:38,270
The quiet. A little flash of the same

36
00:01:38,310 --> 00:01:41,110
embarrassment you felt earlier that day.

37
00:01:42,160 --> 00:01:43,302
But why that memory?

38
00:01:43,342 --> 00:01:45,783
Why not the hundred perfectly nice moments

39
00:01:45,823 --> 00:01:46,965
from the same party?

40
00:01:47,005 --> 00:01:49,565
Part of the answer is that emotional moments

41
00:01:49,605 --> 00:01:52,165
tend to be stored more strongly than neutral

42
00:01:52,205 --> 00:01:54,883
ones. The arousal of the moment helps lock the

43
00:01:54,923 --> 00:01:55,864
memory in.

44
00:01:56,914 --> 00:01:59,519
Embarrassment, though, is a very particular

45
00:01:59,559 --> 00:02:01,611
emotion. In the nineteen nineties

46
00:02:01,651 --> 00:02:03,887
psychologist Mark Leary proposed that

47
00:02:03,927 --> 00:02:05,795
self-esteem works like a gauge...

48
00:02:05,835 --> 00:02:08,625
a sociometer, that tracks how accepted you are

49
00:02:08,665 --> 00:02:09,999
by other people.

50
00:02:11,049 --> 00:02:14,217
In his experiments, being left out of a group

51
00:02:14,257 --> 00:02:16,855
for personal reasons lowered people's

52
00:02:16,895 --> 00:02:20,062
self-esteem. Being left out at random didn't.

53
00:02:20,102 --> 00:02:22,985
The gauge wasn't reacting to being alone.

54
00:02:23,025 --> 00:02:25,799
It was reacting to being rejected.

55
00:02:26,849 --> 00:02:29,441
So, in that view, an awkward moment isn't just

56
00:02:29,481 --> 00:02:31,845
a bad memory. To the gauge, it's a warning

57
00:02:31,885 --> 00:02:33,504
light. Did they like me less?

58
00:02:33,544 --> 00:02:36,079
Am I still in? For a social species, that's a

59
00:02:36,119 --> 00:02:38,472
question the brain takes seriously.

60
00:02:39,522 --> 00:02:41,668
That explains why the memory is kept.

61
00:02:41,708 --> 00:02:44,090
It doesn't explain why it shows up at the

62
00:02:44,130 --> 00:02:45,661
worst possible hour.

63
00:02:49,311 --> 00:02:53,687
Think about what your mind is doing during the

64
00:02:53,727 --> 00:02:57,719
day. Messages, traffic, conversations, the

65
00:02:57,759 --> 00:03:00,695
next thing, and the next thing.

66
00:03:00,735 --> 00:03:04,823
Now think about what it's doing at two a.m.

67
00:03:04,863 --> 00:03:08,279
Nothing. No task. Nobody to talk to.

68
00:03:08,319 --> 00:03:11,165
Just you… and the ceiling.

69
00:03:12,215 --> 00:03:14,598
In twenty ten, Harvard psychologists Matthew

70
00:03:14,638 --> 00:03:16,856
Killingsworth and Daniel Gilbert built an

71
00:03:16,896 --> 00:03:19,334
iPhone app that pinged thousands of people at

72
00:03:19,374 --> 00:03:21,591
random moments and asked three questions.

73
00:03:21,631 --> 00:03:23,574
How do you feel? What are you doing?

74
00:03:23,614 --> 00:03:25,886
And are you thinking about something other

75
00:03:25,926 --> 00:03:27,543
than what you're doing?

76
00:03:28,593 --> 00:03:31,056
In almost half of the samples, forty seven

77
00:03:31,096 --> 00:03:33,798
percent, people's minds had wandered away from

78
00:03:33,838 --> 00:03:35,049
what they were doing.

79
00:03:35,089 --> 00:03:37,314
And when they wandered, people were no

80
00:03:37,354 --> 00:03:39,937
happier, even if the thoughts were pleasant...

81
00:03:39,977 --> 00:03:42,440
and noticeably unhappier when the thoughts

82
00:03:42,480 --> 00:03:44,439
were neutral or unpleasant.

83
00:03:45,489 --> 00:03:48,143
That was during the day, with something to do.

84
00:03:48,183 --> 00:03:50,721
In bed, there's nothing to wander away from.

85
00:03:50,761 --> 00:03:52,361
The mind is completely free...

86
00:03:52,401 --> 00:03:55,094
and it doesn't always go somewhere nice.

87
00:03:56,144 --> 00:03:58,669
The sleep researcher Allison Harvey put this

88
00:03:58,709 --> 00:04:00,885
at the center of a well known model of

89
00:04:00,925 --> 00:04:03,566
insomnia. Worry and rumination at bedtime keep

90
00:04:03,606 --> 00:04:06,248
the mind switched on, which makes it harder to

91
00:04:06,288 --> 00:04:08,813
drift off. And the longer you lie awake, the

92
00:04:08,853 --> 00:04:10,894
more airtime the replay gets.

93
00:04:11,944 --> 00:04:14,038
So two a.m. is the perfect setup

94
00:04:14,078 --> 00:04:16,820
a strongly stored memory, a quiet mind, and

95
00:04:16,860 --> 00:04:18,566
nothing to compete with it.

96
00:04:18,606 --> 00:04:20,441
But here's the real question.

97
00:04:20,481 --> 00:04:23,742
Once it starts… why can't you just let it go?

98
00:04:27,392 --> 00:04:30,111
The psychologist who studied this most was

99
00:04:30,151 --> 00:04:31,491
Susan Nolen-Hoeksema.

100
00:04:31,531 --> 00:04:33,133
She called it rumination

101
00:04:33,173 --> 00:04:36,089
going over and over a bad feeling, its causes

102
00:04:36,129 --> 00:04:37,469
and its consequences...

103
00:04:37,509 --> 00:04:40,881
without moving toward doing anything about it.

104
00:04:41,931 --> 00:04:44,086
In October nineteen eighty nine, a major

105
00:04:44,126 --> 00:04:46,391
earthquake hit the San Francisco Bay Area.

106
00:04:46,431 --> 00:04:48,915
By coincidence, she had measured the moods and

107
00:04:48,955 --> 00:04:51,384
coping styles of a group of students just two

108
00:04:51,424 --> 00:04:53,036
weeks before it struck.

109
00:04:54,086 --> 00:04:56,552
She checked on them again ten days after the

110
00:04:56,592 --> 00:04:58,204
quake, and seven weeks after.

111
00:04:58,244 --> 00:05:00,823
The students who were already more distressed

112
00:05:00,863 --> 00:05:03,329
and who already tended to ruminate, had more

113
00:05:03,369 --> 00:05:05,664
symptoms of depression and stress at both

114
00:05:05,704 --> 00:05:06,624
check-ins.

115
00:05:07,674 --> 00:05:09,250
That's a correlation, not proof.

116
00:05:09,290 --> 00:05:11,422
So she and her colleagues took the question

117
00:05:11,462 --> 00:05:12,469
into the lab.

118
00:05:13,519 --> 00:05:15,669
People who were already feeling low spent a

119
00:05:15,709 --> 00:05:17,655
few minutes either thinking about their

120
00:05:17,695 --> 00:05:19,182
feelings and about themselves...

121
00:05:19,222 --> 00:05:21,270
or thinking about neutral scenes that had

122
00:05:21,310 --> 00:05:22,882
nothing to do with them.

123
00:05:23,932 --> 00:05:26,266
The ruminating group got more down.

124
00:05:26,306 --> 00:05:28,504
The distracted group felt better.

125
00:05:28,544 --> 00:05:31,489
And in a set of studies by Sonja Lyubomirsky

126
00:05:31,529 --> 00:05:34,473
and Nolen-Hoeksema, low mood ruminators came

127
00:05:34,513 --> 00:05:37,390
up with weaker solutions to everyday social

128
00:05:37,430 --> 00:05:39,679
problems than everyone else.

129
00:05:40,729 --> 00:05:43,810
Think about that. The thing that feels like

130
00:05:43,850 --> 00:05:46,786
problem solving… made the problem solving

131
00:05:46,826 --> 00:05:47,612
worse.

132
00:05:48,662 --> 00:05:50,289
So why do we keep doing it?

133
00:05:50,329 --> 00:05:52,204
Partly because it feels useful.

134
00:05:52,244 --> 00:05:54,797
When psychologists Costas Papageorgiou and

135
00:05:54,837 --> 00:05:57,638
Adrian Wells interviewed people with recurring

136
00:05:57,678 --> 00:06:00,232
depression, every single one held positive

137
00:06:00,272 --> 00:06:01,776
beliefs about rumination.

138
00:06:01,816 --> 00:06:03,566
That it helps you understand.

139
00:06:03,606 --> 00:06:05,562
That it's a way of coping.

140
00:06:06,612 --> 00:06:07,720
An honest note here.

141
00:06:07,760 --> 00:06:10,017
In these lab studies, the harm showed up

142
00:06:10,057 --> 00:06:12,659
mainly in people who were already feeling low.

143
00:06:12,699 --> 00:06:15,013
In a good mood, a few minutes of brooding

144
00:06:15,053 --> 00:06:17,425
didn't do much. The trap is strongest when

145
00:06:17,465 --> 00:06:18,573
you're already down...

146
00:06:18,613 --> 00:06:21,547
and tired, in the dark, it's easy to be down.

147
00:06:22,597 --> 00:06:25,426
So is the answer to just stop thinking about

148
00:06:25,466 --> 00:06:28,165
it? Not quite. Because the next experiment

149
00:06:28,205 --> 00:06:30,316
found something surprising.

150
00:06:33,966 --> 00:06:36,941
In two thousand five, Ethan Kross, Ozlem Ayduk

151
00:06:36,981 --> 00:06:39,891
and Walter Mischel asked students to recall a

152
00:06:39,931 --> 00:06:42,514
time they had felt overwhelming anger at

153
00:06:42,554 --> 00:06:43,428
someone.

154
00:06:44,478 --> 00:06:45,817
Then they split them up.

155
00:06:45,857 --> 00:06:48,344
Some were told to go back to that moment and

156
00:06:48,384 --> 00:06:50,986
relive it, as if it were happening to them all

157
00:06:51,026 --> 00:06:53,399
over again. Others were told to take a few

158
00:06:53,439 --> 00:06:55,984
steps back… and watch the scene unfold, as if

159
00:06:56,024 --> 00:06:58,339
it were happening to a distant version of

160
00:06:58,379 --> 00:06:59,361
themselves.

161
00:07:00,411 --> 00:07:02,711
And within each group, some focused on what

162
00:07:02,751 --> 00:07:05,214
they were feeling… and others on why they felt

163
00:07:05,254 --> 00:07:06,094
that way.

164
00:07:07,144 --> 00:07:09,239
Now, asking why is supposed to be the

165
00:07:09,279 --> 00:07:11,720
dangerous part. It's the classic rumination

166
00:07:11,760 --> 00:07:14,085
question. And for the people reliving the

167
00:07:14,125 --> 00:07:15,874
scene up close, it didn't help.

168
00:07:15,914 --> 00:07:18,412
But the people who asked why from a distance

169
00:07:18,452 --> 00:07:21,066
felt less anger and less negative emotion than

170
00:07:21,106 --> 00:07:22,264
everyone else.

171
00:07:23,314 --> 00:07:26,172
When the researchers read what people had

172
00:07:26,212 --> 00:07:29,000
written, there was a difference in kind.

173
00:07:29,040 --> 00:07:32,111
Up close, people mostly recounted the scene

174
00:07:32,151 --> 00:07:35,221
what was said, what they felt, blow by blow.

175
00:07:35,261 --> 00:07:37,837
From a distance, they reconstrued it.

176
00:07:37,877 --> 00:07:39,392
They made sense of it...

177
00:07:39,432 --> 00:07:42,398
and some described a kind of closure.

178
00:07:43,448 --> 00:07:45,898
That's the turn. The problem isn't that you

179
00:07:45,938 --> 00:07:47,230
think about the memory.

180
00:07:47,270 --> 00:07:49,777
It's that you think about it from inside it

181
00:07:49,817 --> 00:07:51,920
with the sound turned all the way up.

182
00:07:51,960 --> 00:07:53,931
Same memory… different seat.

183
00:07:54,981 --> 00:07:57,682
Psychologist Edward Watkins, at the University

184
00:07:57,722 --> 00:07:59,768
of Exeter, found a related pattern.

185
00:07:59,808 --> 00:08:02,270
Big, judging questions, like why does this

186
00:08:02,310 --> 00:08:04,892
always happen to me, tend to keep a low mood

187
00:08:04,932 --> 00:08:07,514
going. Concrete questions, like how did that

188
00:08:07,554 --> 00:08:10,195
actually unfold, and what can I do next, tend

189
00:08:10,235 --> 00:08:11,062
to help.

190
00:08:12,112 --> 00:08:14,605
In one of his studies with depressed patients

191
00:08:14,645 --> 00:08:17,138
concrete thinking about their situation led to

192
00:08:17,178 --> 00:08:19,616
better problem solving than abstract brooding

193
00:08:19,656 --> 00:08:20,502
about it.

194
00:08:21,552 --> 00:08:23,938
To be fair, these effects are modest, they

195
00:08:23,978 --> 00:08:26,422
come mostly from lab studies with students

196
00:08:26,462 --> 00:08:29,080
and much of the work comes from a few research

197
00:08:29,120 --> 00:08:31,564
groups. But the direction keeps showing up

198
00:08:31,604 --> 00:08:33,874
questions that judge you keep you stuck.

199
00:08:33,914 --> 00:08:35,203
Questions that observe...

200
00:08:35,243 --> 00:08:35,882
help.

201
00:08:39,532 --> 00:08:42,336
So, next time the replay starts at two a.m.

202
00:08:42,376 --> 00:08:45,309
here are three things to try, each one tied to

203
00:08:45,349 --> 00:08:46,539
the research.

204
00:08:47,589 --> 00:08:50,720
One. Step back. Picture the moment from across

205
00:08:50,760 --> 00:08:53,616
the room, like a fly on the wall, watching

206
00:08:53,656 --> 00:08:55,891
someone who looks a lot like you.

207
00:08:55,931 --> 00:08:59,177
Ask what that observer would actually see.

208
00:09:00,227 --> 00:09:02,096
You can do it with words, too.

209
00:09:02,136 --> 00:09:04,577
In two thousand fourteen, Kross and his

210
00:09:04,617 --> 00:09:07,504
colleagues had students prepare a speech under

211
00:09:07,544 --> 00:09:10,304
pressure. Before speaking, some reflected on

212
00:09:10,344 --> 00:09:12,213
their nerves using the word I.

213
00:09:12,253 --> 00:09:14,830
Others used their own name, or you.

214
00:09:15,880 --> 00:09:18,795
The name group felt less distressed, judges

215
00:09:18,835 --> 00:09:21,887
rated their speeches as better, and afterward

216
00:09:21,927 --> 00:09:24,773
they spent less time beating themselves up

217
00:09:24,813 --> 00:09:27,796
about it. So instead of, why did I say that...

218
00:09:27,836 --> 00:09:29,514
try, by name: okay, Alex.

219
00:09:29,554 --> 00:09:31,897
What actually happened there?

220
00:09:32,947 --> 00:09:34,486
Two. Swap why me for how.

221
00:09:34,526 --> 00:09:37,391
What exactly happened, and is there anything I

222
00:09:37,431 --> 00:09:38,654
want to do about it?

223
00:09:38,694 --> 00:09:41,496
If there is, like a message, an apology, or a

224
00:09:41,536 --> 00:09:44,085
note for next time, write it down for the

225
00:09:44,125 --> 00:09:44,980
morning.

226
00:09:46,030 --> 00:09:48,431
In a sleep lab study at Baylor University

227
00:09:48,471 --> 00:09:51,046
people who spent five minutes writing a to-do

228
00:09:51,086 --> 00:09:53,720
list before bed fell asleep faster than people

229
00:09:53,760 --> 00:09:56,335
who wrote about things they had already done.

230
00:09:56,375 --> 00:09:58,893
Getting it onto paper seems to help the mind

231
00:09:58,933 --> 00:09:59,980
put it down.

232
00:10:01,030 --> 00:10:04,120
Three. If the loop still won't stop, give your

233
00:10:04,160 --> 00:10:07,250
mind something else to hold for a few minutes.

234
00:10:07,290 --> 00:10:09,971
In the distraction experiments, a short

235
00:10:10,011 --> 00:10:12,625
absorbing distraction lifted low moods.

236
00:10:12,665 --> 00:10:15,347
It isn't hiding from the memory forever.

237
00:10:15,387 --> 00:10:18,477
It's letting the alarm settle, so you can look

238
00:10:18,517 --> 00:10:21,044
at it later… from a better seat.

239
00:10:22,094 --> 00:10:24,284
And remember the last episode

240
00:10:24,324 --> 00:10:27,331
the other person almost certainly noticed

241
00:10:27,371 --> 00:10:30,527
less, and judged you less harshly, than you

242
00:10:30,567 --> 00:10:33,574
think. Your replay is in high definition.

243
00:10:33,614 --> 00:10:36,993
Theirs, if it exists at all, is a tiny, blurry

244
00:10:37,033 --> 00:10:38,498
little picture.

245
00:10:39,548 --> 00:10:41,969
If the replay is constant, if it drags your

246
00:10:42,009 --> 00:10:44,545
mood down for weeks, or keeps you awake night

247
00:10:44,585 --> 00:10:47,121
after night, that's worth talking to a doctor

248
00:10:47,161 --> 00:10:48,323
or a therapist about.

249
00:10:48,363 --> 00:10:50,899
Rumination is something they know how to help

250
00:10:50,939 --> 00:10:51,575
with.

251
00:10:52,625 --> 00:10:54,133
So, back to two a.m.

252
00:10:54,173 --> 00:10:55,914
The ceiling. The party.

253
00:10:55,954 --> 00:10:57,775
The thing you said.

254
00:10:58,825 --> 00:11:01,163
Your brain held on to that memory because it

255
00:11:01,203 --> 00:11:02,352
cares about belonging.

256
00:11:02,392 --> 00:11:04,839
That's not a flaw. But you don't have to watch

257
00:11:04,879 --> 00:11:06,028
it from the front row.

258
00:11:06,068 --> 00:11:08,460
Step back… and it's just a scene, from a long

259
00:11:08,500 --> 00:11:10,676
time ago, starring someone you've already

260
00:11:10,716 --> 00:11:11,661
grown past.

261
00:11:12,711 --> 00:11:15,283
What's the moment your brain loves to replay?

262
00:11:15,323 --> 00:11:17,372
You don't have to share the details.

263
00:11:17,412 --> 00:11:19,578
Just tell me how many years old it is.

264
00:11:19,618 --> 00:11:22,074
And if this helped, subscribe for more ways

265
00:11:22,114 --> 00:11:24,263
your brain gets in its own way.
